[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    27[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Findings
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    28[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    29[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `bitwriter_add` performs **no validation whatsoever**:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    30[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    31[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * no `assert`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    32[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * no null-pointer check on `bw`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    33[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * no range check on `bits` (the header documents no range; nothing rejects
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    34[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `bits == 0`, `bits > 64`, or `bits == UINT32_MAX`)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    35[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * no bounds check against `bw->len` / `bw->pos` (those fields are never read
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    36[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   or written by this function)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    37[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * no error enum, no `-1`, no `NULL` return, no `errno`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    38[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * exactly one `return` statement: `return 0`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    39[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    40[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The only guard in the function is the `i < 100` loop cap, which is a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    41[0m:[38;5;10m[49m *termination* bound, not an input rejection: it silently truncates the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    42[0m:[38;5;10m[49m bit-consuming loop instead of signalling an error.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    43[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    44[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Therefore the "rejection" rows below are the *complete* set of ways the C code
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    45[0m:[38;5;10m[49m can be fed invalid/out-of-range input, together with what the C **actually
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    46[0m:[38;5;10m[49m does** (which is the contract the Rust must reproduce). Each row is covered by
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    47[0m:[38;5;10m[49m a differential test in `tests/differential.rs`.
